<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bartatua</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> · Also known as Protothyes, Partatua</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bartatua was a Scythian king of the 7th century BCE known from Assyrian texts and usually identified with Protothyes, named by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> as the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madyes">Madyes</a>. He asked the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> for the hand of an Assyrian princess, and his people thereafter generally sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>. Some scholars associate the rich burial at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziwiye">Ziwiye</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> with him.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The Akkadian name Partatua or Bartatua and the Greek Protothues come from a Scythian name meaning either with far-reaching strength or mighty in battle. In the 8th and 7th centuries BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, pushed west by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massagetae">Massagetae</a> or the Issedones, drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a> from the Caspian Steppe and moved south along the Caspian into the region of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, which became their base in Western Asia until the early 6th century BCE, while their kings kept their headquarters in the steppes north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Bartatua succeeded Išpakaia, perhaps his father, after Išpakaia died in battle against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> around 676 BCE. In the later mid-670s his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>, threatened the Assyrian provinces of Parsumaš and Bīt Ḫamban and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>'s links with its vassal Ḫubuškia. The Assyrians had opened talks with him at once, and in 672 BCE his request for a daughter of Esarhaddon was put to the oracle of the sun god Šamaš.</p><p>The texts do not record whether the marriage took place, but the close alliance that followed suggests it did. It would have been the first marriage of an Assyrian king's daughter to a nomadic lord, and it made Bartatua a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> holding his lands as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of the Assyrian king. In 660 to 659 BCE, after the Mannaean king Aḫsēri was overthrown and killed, his son Ualli asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> for help, which came through the Scythian king, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> then extended their hegemony over Mannai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The alliance with the Assyrian dynasty and the nearness of Mannai and Urartu brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> under strong Assyrian cultural influence. Bartatua was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madyes">Madyes</a>, under whom Scythian power in Western Asia reached its peak. Greek and Roman writers merged Bartatua, his predecessors and his successors into one king who drove out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a>, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> and the Egyptian king Sesostris, a figure of legend, and ruled Asia for many years, later calling him Idanthyrsos or Tanausis. One theory identifies him with Paroyr Skayordi, an Armenian king of legend named in the history of Movses Khorenatsi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bartatua” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Scythians enter written history in Assyrian texts of the 670s BCE, when King Esarhaddon asked the sun god whether he should give a daughter in marriage to <b>Bartatua</b>, king of the Ishkuzai, usually identified with Herodotus’s Protothyes.</blockquote><a href="/culture/scythians">Scythians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Their king <b>Bartatua</b>, identified with Herodotus' Protothyes, sought an Assyrian princess in marriage, and the Scythians thereafter generally supported Assyria against the Medes and Cimmerians.</blockquote><a href="/state/ishkuza">Ishkuza</a></li></ul></section>
